  "why": "The statement asks for a classification of pairs under simultaneous conjugation, which in the wild-problem sense means an explicit description of the orbits (normal forms or a parametrisation). The separating-invariant results (Derksen et al. 2021; Bongartz-Friedland 2026) answer a changed question, deciding equivalence rather than describing the orbits. Bongartz and Friedland say outright that their stratification 'is not the solution to this problem', so both are variant. Qiao-Sun's canonical-form algorithm over finite fields was the closest call: one could read it as a finite-field case, but the authors disclaim classifying pairs and an algorithmic canonical form is a different notion from classification, so variant. Bongartz 2025 rules out one kind of normal form, which bounds nothing and classifies nothing, so none. Counting, polynomial similarity, centralizers and variety geometry are variants. Subclass classifications that predate the snapshot (Hua, BFPS, Bondarenko-Zubaruk) are cases. The 1983 and 2003 refereed papers and the 2020 journal paper are digested via journal acceptance, as is FOCS acceptance. No AI was involved in any event.",
